<h2>Memory Poisoning Vectors</h2>

<div class="threat-card critical">
  <h4>Indirect Long-Term Memory Injection</h4>
  <p>Attackers inject instructions through secondary document sources (like Wiki edits, calendar invites, or PDFs) that the agent reads and saves into its permanent profiling databases.</p>
</div>

<div class="threat-card high">
  <h4>Vector Database / RAG Poisoning</h4>
  <p>Inserting documents loaded with semantic smuggling prompts into the vector store. When the agent queries the vector DB, it retrieves the poisoned document, overriding the system context.</p>
</div>

<div class="threat-card medium">
  <h4>User Profile Tampering</h4>
  <p>Exploiting user feedback loops to modify the agent's stored preferences (e.g., setting the user's default browser tool to a malicious URL helper).</p>
</div>

<StepWalkthrough
  title="Securing Agent Memory Systems"
  steps={[
    { title: "Enforce Semantic Validation on Write", content: "Before writing any observation to long-term memory, pass the content through a validation step. Filter out programmatic structures, command patterns, or imperative instructions.", icon: "🔍" },
    { title: "Partition Memory Spaces", content: "Implement strict user-boundary separation for memory databases. An agent reading context for User A must never retrieve memory snippets or documents generated by User B.", icon: "🧱" },
    { title: "Define Memory Lifecycle and Expiry", content: "Set decay rates and validation parameters for stored memories. Automatically purge unused state history and enforce mandatory re-verification for high-risk stored facts.", icon: "⏱️" },
    { title: "Strict Sanitization on Retrieval", content: "Treat retrieved memories as untrusted data. When loading memories into the active system context, wrap them in clear semantic boundary tags (e.g., `<memory_context>` tags) and instruct the model not to execute commands found within them.", icon: "🛡️" },
  ]}
/>

<div class="defense-section">
  <h3>Memory Security Rules</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>No Direct Database Updates.</strong> Agents should never execute raw database inserts or updates. All memory writes must pass through a strict data access API layer.</li>
    <li><strong>Instruction vs Data Separation.</strong> Clearly tag memory variables within prompts so the LLM parses retrieved records strictly as passive text, not active operations.</li>
    <li><strong>Regular Cleanups.</strong> Periodically audit agent memory databases using classification models to find and delete anomalous rules or persistent injection payloads.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

